<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">Any of these works. The APY differences are minimal (4.25% vs. 4.40% on \$10,000 = \$15/year). Pick the one with the cleanest interface or the one you already have a relationship with.</p>
<h2 style="font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 3rem 0 1.5rem; color: var(--color-text);">How to Switch (10-Minute Process)</h2>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>Step 1: Open a high-yield account (5 minutes)</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">Go to Marcus, Ally, or Amex. Click &quot;Open Account.&quot; Provide:</p>
<ul style="margin: 1.5rem 0; padding-left: 1.5rem;">
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">Name, address, SSN</li>
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">Funding source (your current checking account routing/account numbers)</li>
</ul>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">You&#39;ll get approved instantly (soft credit pull, doesn&#39;t affect your score).</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>Step 2: Transfer your savings (2 minutes)</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">Link your current bank to the new HYSA. Initiate a transfer for however much you want to move (\$5,000? \$10,000? All of it?).</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>Step 3: Wait 2-3 business days</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">The transfer clears. Your money is now earning 4.5% instead of 0.01%.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>Step 4: Set up recurring deposits (3 minutes)</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">If you auto-save each month (\$200? \$500?), update your direct deposit or auto-transfer to route into the HYSA instead of your old savings.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">Done. You&#39;re now earning \$400+/year more than you were last week.</p>
<h2 style="font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 3rem 0 1.5rem; color: var(--color-text);">What to Do With the Extra Interest</h2>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">If you&#39;re earning an extra \$400/year from a \$10,000 balance, here&#39;s what you can do with it:</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>Option 1: Let it compound</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">Leave the interest in the account. Next year, you&#39;re earning 4.5% on \$10,450 instead of \$10,000. Over 10 years, that&#39;s an extra \$5,500 in interest (vs. \$10 at Chase).</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>Option 2: Use it to fund another goal</strong></p>
<ul style="margin: 1.5rem 0; padding-left: 1.5rem;">
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">\$400/year = \$33/month, which covers a cheap gym membership</li>
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">Or a monthly subscription service (Netflix, Spotify, etc.)</li>
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">Or an extra debt payment</li>
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">Or an annual Roth IRA contribution boost</li>
</ul>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>Option 3: Reinvest it</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">Take the \$400 and drop it into an index fund. Over 30 years at 8% average returns, that single \$400 becomes \$4,200.</p>
<h2 style="font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 3rem 0 1.5rem; color: var(--color-text);">Advanced Move: Stacking HYSAs With Credit Cards</h2>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">This is where it gets interesting.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">If you&#39;re using a 2% cashback card for all your spending, here&#39;s the cycle:</p>
<ol>
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">Spend \$2,000/month on your 2% card → earn \$40/month cashback</li>
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">Pay off the card from your high-yield savings (keeps your checking clean)</li>
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">Cashback accumulates in the HYSA, earning 4.5%</li>
<li style="margin: 0.5rem 0;">At the end of the year, you&#39;ve earned \$480 in cashback PLUS \$20+ in interest on the cashback balance</li>
</ol>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">You&#39;re earning interest on your cashback. It&#39;s a small optimization, but it&#39;s free.</p>
<h2 style="font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 3rem 0 1.5rem; color: var(--color-text);">When NOT to Use a High-Yield Account</h2>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">There are exactly two scenarios where a traditional savings account makes sense:</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>1. You need instant cash access multiple times per week</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">If you&#39;re constantly moving money in and out, a HYSA&#39;s 1-2 day transfer time is annoying. But this describes almost no one&#39;s actual emergency fund usage.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>2. You have under \$1,000 in savings</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">At \$1,000, the difference between 0.01% and 4.5% is \$45/year. Still worth it, but if the mental overhead of managing another account isn&#39;t worth \$45 to you, fine.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">If you have \$1,000 or less, your priority should be building the emergency fund to \$3,000-6,000, not optimizing interest. But once you&#39;re there, move it.</p>
<h2 style="font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 3rem 0 1.5rem; color: var(--color-text);">The Psychology of Seeing Your Money Grow</h2>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">Here&#39;s an underrated benefit of HYSAs: you can <em>watch</em> your money grow.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">At Chase, your \$10,000 earns \$0.08/month. You don&#39;t notice it.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">At a HYSA, your \$10,000 earns \$37.50/month. You see it. It&#39;s tangible. Every month, there&#39;s free money showing up.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">This creates a positive feedback loop. You&#39;re more likely to keep saving when you see the balance grow faster than your contributions alone.</p>
<h2 style="font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 3rem 0 1.5rem; color: var(--color-text);">The Bottom Line</h2>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">If your savings are sitting in a traditional bank account earning 0.01%, you&#39;re giving the bank a \$400+/year gift. They&#39;re lending your money out at 7-18% and paying you nothing.</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">High-yield accounts pay 4.25-4.50%. Same FDIC insurance. Same safety. Same accessibility (with a 1-2 day transfer delay).</p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;"><strong>The fix takes 10 minutes. You&#39;ll never think about it again. And you&#39;ll earn an extra \$400-1,000/year depending on your balance.</strong></p>
<p style="margin: 1rem 0;">If you do one thing this week, move your emergency fund to a high-yield account.</p>
